# check=skip=FromPlatformFlagConstDisallowed
#
# ^ A BuildKit parser directive, and it MUST be the first line of the file --
#   directives are only recognised before any comment or instruction.
#
#   It silences exactly one advisory lint, `FromPlatformFlagConstDisallowed`,
#   which suggests `FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM` instead of a literal. That
#   advice is written for images intended to build for many architectures; this
#   one is deliberately single-architecture, because the oracle it serves is
#   compared byte-for-byte against a Python implementation whose dependency
#   closure is x86_64 and against COBOL numeric layouts that are
#   architecture-dependent. `$BUILDPLATFORM` would make the arbiter's
#   architecture a property of whoever ran the build -- the exact drift the pin
#   exists to prevent. The lint is therefore skipped by name, in one place, with
#   this reason recorded, rather than left to be re-litigated as noise on every
#   build. Nothing else is skipped: every other check must stay clean.
#

# =============================================================================
#  harness/Dockerfile.mariadb
#  The MariaDB half of the compiled-COBOL ORACLE for the ACAS posting-cycle
#  migration (COBOL -> Python 3.12).
# =============================================================================
#
#  PURPOSE
#  -------
#  This image serves ONE database to BOTH sides of the parity comparison: the
#  compiled COBOL cycle and the migrated Python cycle. It exists so that, for a
#  given scenario, the ordering-normalised diff of the affected tables after a
#  Python run versus a COBOL run against an identical seed is EMPTY.
#
#  Because both sides are served by this single, identically-configured image,
#  a non-empty diff is always a real behavioural difference and never an
#  artefact of the comparison. That property is the whole point of the image,
#  and it is why this Dockerfile is deliberately, aggressively boring: it pins
#  a server-level `autocommit=1` -- the only mode in which the frozen COBOL
#  writes durable rows, proven from the frozen source below -- and applies the
#  frozen schema. Nothing else.
#
#  POSITION IN THE TREE
#  --------------------
#  `harness/` is the compiled-COBOL oracle tree and is a SIBLING of
#  `acas_posting/`, never a sub-package -- annotated in the plan as "the
#  compiled oracle; NEVER on the package import path". There is no
#  `harness/__init__.py` and nothing here is importable: `pyproject.toml`
#  packages `acas_posting*` only and excludes `harness*`.
#  That separation is the structural expression of R-1 (no COBOL at runtime) --
#  the shipped Python artifact carries no COBOL, no `cobc` requirement and no
#  linkage to the bridge's C interface object.
#
#  This image is the DATABASE half of the harness. The COBOL toolchain
#  (GnuCOBOL, `presql2`, `cobmysqlapi.o`, the MySQL client libraries, ncurses)
#  lives EXCLUSIVELY in the sibling `harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol`. Keeping the
#  two images disjoint is R-1 enforced at the container layer: grep this file
#  and you will find no COBOL toolchain component of any kind.
#
#  BUILD CONTEXT -- READ THIS BEFORE BUILDING
#  ------------------------------------------
#  The build context MUST be the REPOSITORY ROOT, not `harness/`, because the
#  frozen schema is copied from `mysql/ACASDB.sql` -- a path that only resolves
#  relative to the repository root. `harness/docker-compose.yml` declares
#  `context: ..` with `dockerfile: harness/Dockerfile.mariadb` precisely so the
#  copy resolves.
#
#      Preferred:  docker compose -f harness/docker-compose.yml build mariadb
#      Equivalent: docker build -f harness/Dockerfile.mariadb \
#                      -t acas-oracle-mariadb .        # <-- run from repo root
#
#  Building with `harness/` as the context FAILS at the COPY, by design. That
#  failure is a feature: it makes the contract self-enforcing rather than a
#  matter of documentation.
#
#  VERSION PROVENANCE -- REPOSITORY EVIDENCE, NOT A PREFERENCE
#  ----------------------------------------------------------
#  The server version is taken from the frozen schema dump's own header:
#      [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1] -- MariaDB dump 10.19  Distrib 10.11.7-MariaDB, ...
#      [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L5] -- Server version   10.11.7-MariaDB
#  and the database name likewise:
#      [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L3] -- Host: localhost    Database: ACASDB
#  The tag is therefore pinned EXACTLY to `10.11.7`. No `10.11`, no `10`, no
#  `latest`, no floating tag: the oracle must be the system as the repository
#  specifies it, and a drifting base image would silently change the arbiter.
#
#  THE ONLY DDL THIS IMAGE EVER EXECUTES (R-3)
#  -------------------------------------------
#  `mysql/ACASDB.sql`, applied BYTE-FOR-BYTE, unmodified. The file is COPYied
#  into `/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/` and handed to the vendor entrypoint
#  unchanged. It is never piped through `sed`, `awk`, `tr`, `iconv` or any
#  other filter; no modified copy is vendored into `harness/`; and no index,
#  view, trigger, stored procedure, constraint, column or table is added.
#  A build-time SHA-256 tripwire plus five structural invariants make that
#  guarantee machine-checked rather than asserted -- see the verification step
#  below. No migration tooling (Alembic or otherwise) is present or needed:
#  the schema is frozen, so there is nothing legitimate for one to do.
#
#  Reviewers can confirm the copy independently:
#      docker run --rm --entrypoint sha256sum <image> \
#          /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/10-ACASDB.sql
#      sha256sum mysql/ACASDB.sql          # must print the identical digest
#
#  THE CHARSET CAVEAT IS PRESERVED, NOT FIXED (R-4)
#  ------------------------------------------------
#  The maintainer flags a charset concern in his own words at
#  [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L9-L11]:
#      "THERE IS NOT ANY DATA RECORDS PRESENT HERE"
#      "YOU MAY NEED TO CHANGE the defined Character set in all tables"
#      "TO MATCH ANY OF YOUR REQUIREMENTS IF THEY DIFFER"
#  The dump is then internally inconsistent: its preamble sets
#  `SET NAMES utf8mb4` [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L16] and each table is preceded by
#  `SET character_set_client = utf8` [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L30], yet all 33 tables
#  are created `DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3 COLLATE=utf8mb3_general_ci`
#  [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L37].
#
#  This image does NOT harmonise that. No `character-set-server`, no
#  `collation-server`, no per-table override. A defect reproduced is correct;
#  a defect fixed is a failure. The oracle must behave exactly as the
#  maintainer's schema makes it behave, so `utf8mb3_general_ci` is asserted as
#  an INVARIANT below rather than corrected.
#
#  DETERMINISM (R-6)
#  -----------------
#  Nothing time-, host- or build-dependent is baked into this image: no build
#  date, no timestamp label, no VCS revision, no run identifier, and no server
#  setting that could make one run differ from the next. The determinism suite
#  requires two runs of the same scenario under the same pinned clock to
#  produce byte-identical dumps, and `SYSTEM-REC` carries a diff-visible
#  `Run-Date` column [copybooks/wssystem.cob:L67], so any clock leakage here
#  would corrupt the evidence. The run date reaches the programs purely through
#  linkage from the controlled clock, never from this container.
#
#  NO SEED DATA
#  ------------
#  The dump carries no rows -- [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L9] says so outright. All data
#  arrives via `harness/seed.sh` driving the maintainer's own `common/*LD.cbl`
#  loaders. Those loaders reach the server through the very same
#  `cobmysqlapi.o` interface the posting bridges use and -- as the SERVER
#  CONFIGURATION section below establishes from the frozen source -- neither the
#  loaders nor the bridges ever execute a REACHABLE COMMIT. Seeded rows and
#  posted rows are consequently durable under exactly one server setting,
#  `autocommit=1`, and that setting is a property of this SERVER rather than of
#  one script precisely so that every client session -- the COBOL loaders, the
#  COBOL bridges via `cobmysqlapi.o`, the `mariadb` CLI used by `seed.sh` and
#  `reset_db.sh`, and the Python driver used by `dump_tables.py` -- inherits it
#  identically. If they did not, the two sides of the diff would not be
#  comparable.
#
#  CREDENTIALS
#  -----------
#  NONE are hard-coded here, and no credential value appears anywhere in this
#  file -- not in an ENV, not in an ARG, not in a comment. They arrive at run
#  time from `harness/docker-compose.yml` as `MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD`,
#  `MARIADB_USER` and `MARIADB_PASSWORD`.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Base image -- EXACT tag from [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1] and [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L5],
#  PINNED BY CONTENT DIGEST, AND PINNED TO ONE ARCHITECTURE
#
#  THE TAG IS NOT ENOUGH. `mariadb:10.11.7` names a version but not a build: the
#  publisher may repoint that tag at a rebuilt image with different OpenSSL,
#  glibc or entrypoint content, and two runs of the same repository revision
#  would then be arbitrated by two different servers. The digest below is the
#  OCI image INDEX digest of the tag as resolved and verified on this host, so
#  the tag now serves purely as human-readable documentation while the digest
#  decides what is actually pulled. If the digest ever fails to resolve, the
#  build fails loudly instead of silently substituting a different arbiter.
#
#  THE ARCHITECTURE IS NOT ENOUGH EITHER. The comparison protocol diffs table
#  state produced by COMPILED COBOL on one side, and COBOL numeric storage is
#  representation-sensitive: `COMP`/`BINARY-LONG` fields have a byte order and a
#  width, `COMP-3` is packed decimal, and the bridge's C interface
#  [presql2-package/cobmysqlapi38.c] moves those bytes through host variables.
#  The Python closure the harness installs is likewise resolved for x86_64. A
#  build that silently produced an arm64 oracle would be comparing two different
#  machines, so the platform is fixed here rather than inherited from whatever
#  host happens to run the build. `linux/amd64` is not an arbitrary choice: the
#  frozen dump's own header records the producing server as
#  "for Linux (x86_64)" [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1], and the index digest above does
#  contain a linux/amd64 manifest.
#
#  Both pins are deliberately NOT build arguments. An overrideable pin is not a
#  pin -- see the R-3 TRIPWIRE section for the same argument applied to the
#  schema checksum.
#
#  RESOLVING THE DIGEST. The value below is the MULTI-ARCH INDEX digest, read
#  with
#
#      docker buildx imagetools inspect mariadb:10.11.7
#
#  and deliberately not the per-PLATFORM manifest digest that
#  `docker manifest inspect --verbose` reports -- a trap worth naming, because
#  both values look equally plausible and only one of them is the index. The
#  architecture is fixed by the `--platform` flag above rather than by narrowing
#  the digest, so the pin stays readable against the published index.
#
#  MOVING THE SERVER VERSION requires changing BOTH the tag and the digest, and
#  the version is not a free choice: [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1] and
#  [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L5] record the server the frozen dump was produced by.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FROM --platform=linux/amd64 mariadb:10.11.7@sha256:27292753bb759cd22be72e3c8192f1259ef47010c8634b5da0bc886845efe9fd


#  Metadata. Descriptive only. Deliberately carries NO created/revision/
#  timestamp label: those change on every build and would break the
#  byte-identical-dump guarantee this tree exists to provide (R-6).
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.title="ACAS posting-cycle oracle - MariaDB" \
      org.opencontainers.image.description="MariaDB 10.11.7 serving the frozen ACASDB schema with autocommit enabled, matching the sole mode in which the frozen COBOL loaders and bridges write durable rows, for COBOL-vs-Python posting-cycle parity comparison." \
      org.opencontainers.image.base.name="docker.io/library/mariadb:10.11.7@sha256:27292753bb759cd22be72e3c8192f1259ef47010c8634b5da0bc886845efe9fd" \
      org.opencontainers.image.base.digest="sha256:27292753bb759cd22be72e3c8192f1259ef47010c8634b5da0bc886845efe9fd" \
      acas.harness.platform="linux/amd64" \
      org.opencontainers.image.licenses="GPL-3.0-or-later" \
      acas.harness.role="oracle-database" \
      acas.harness.schema.source="mysql/ACASDB.sql" \
      acas.harness.schema.frozen="true" \
      acas.harness.autocommit="1" \
      acas.harness.cobol.toolchain="absent"


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  SERVER CONFIGURATION -- autocommit MUST be ON
#
#  This is the single most important behavioural setting in the image, and the
#  reasoning behind it is counter-intuitive enough to be set out in full,
#  because the maintainer's own loader headers appear at first reading to demand
#  the opposite.
#
#  WHAT THE LOADER HEADERS SAY
#  ---------------------------
#  [common/glbatchLD.cbl:L9-L13], verbatim:
#
#      *>  This modules uses commit and rollback so *
#      *>  you MUST ensure that autocommit is OFF   *
#      *>   in the rdb settings. It is as default   *
#      *>   set ON.                                 *
#
#  That header is NOT unique to `glbatchLD`. Verified by census over the 28
#  `common/*LD.cbl` loaders: 17 carry this identical four-line banner (e.g.
#  [common/analLD.cbl:L10-L13]) and ALL 28 reference autocommit somewhere.
#
#  WHAT THE LOADER CODE ACTUALLY DOES
#  ----------------------------------
#  The banner describes an INTENTION that the shipped code does not carry out.
#  Every loader declares a `aa020-Rollback` paragraph calling
#  "MySQL_rollback" and a `aa030-Commit` paragraph calling "MySQL_commit", and
#  in the frozen source BOTH are unreachable:
#
#    * every `perform aa020-Rollback` is commented out with the `*>` marker --
#      [common/glbatchLD.cbl:L386], [common/glbatchLD.cbl:L428],
#      [common/nominalLD.cbl:L386], [common/nominalLD.cbl:L428],
#      [common/nominalLD.cbl:L473], [common/slpostingLD.cbl:L363],
#      [common/slpostingLD.cbl:L405], [common/slpostingLD.cbl:L450];
#    * `aa030-Commit` has ZERO `perform` sites of any kind, commented or not,
#      in any of the 28 loaders -- the mainline falls through
#      `go to aa010-Read` / `go to aa999-Finish` and never reaches it.
#
#  Census command, run over the frozen tree, which returns NO matches:
#      grep -n '^ *perform.*\(aa020\|aa030\|Commit\|Rollback\)' common/*LD.cbl
#
#  The maintainer records the consequence himself, immediately above the dead
#  paragraphs:
#      [common/analLD.cbl:L377]  "otherwise as normally it is set to autocommit !!!!!"
#      [common/glbatchLD.cbl:L453] and [common/analLD.cbl:L442]
#                                "These do not work during testing with mariadb -
#                                 Non transactional model or autocommit set ON"
#      [common/finalLD.cbl:L361] "which can be ignored unless you thought
#                                 autocommit was set up."
#
#  THE POSTING SIDE NEVER COMMITS EITHER
#  -------------------------------------
#  The same census over the code path the parity comparison actually exercises:
#    * ZERO occurrences of COMMIT, ROLLBACK or START TRANSACTION in any of the
#      twenty in-scope bridges (e.g. [common/glpostingMT.cbl],
#      [common/nominalMT.cbl], [common/glbatchMT.cbl]);
#    * ZERO in the in-scope handlers, including [common/acas007.cbl],
#      [common/acas008.cbl] and [common/acasirsub4.cbl];
#    * ZERO on any bridge close path -- `MySQL_close` is called directly;
#    * the C interface `cobmysqlapi38.c` (vendored in `presql2-latest.zip`)
#      EXPOSES `MySQL_commit` but never calls it internally, and its
#      `mysql_real_connect` never alters the session autocommit mode.
#
#  WHY THAT FORCES autocommit=1
#  ----------------------------
#  Under `autocommit=0` MariaDB opens an implicit transaction on the first DML
#  statement of every session and discards it if the client disconnects without
#  committing. Since NO reachable COMMIT exists anywhere in the loader family,
#  the bridges, the handlers or the C interface, `autocommit=0` makes every
#  COBOL write -- seeding AND posting alike -- vanish at session close, while
#  the Python side commits (`autocommit=True` in
#  `acas_posting/dal/connection.py`) and keeps its rows. The state diff would
#  then report a difference on every single row of every scenario, caused
#  entirely by the harness rather than by the migration. That is precisely the
#  comparison artefact R-6 forbids.
#
#  Verified empirically against this exact base image: with the server started
#  `--autocommit=0`, an INSERT followed by a clean disconnect and no COMMIT --
#  which is literally what every ACAS loader and bridge does -- leaves a fresh
#  session reading ZERO rows; with `autocommit=1` the identical sequence leaves
#  the row present.
#
#  RELATIONSHIP TO THE AAP
#  -----------------------
#  The AAP scopes the loader requirement to the seeding window: "autocommit must
#  be OFF **during seeding**" (§0.5.2, and §0.4.1.7's derivation from
#  [common/glbatchLD.cbl:L9-L13]). Because the shipped loaders' commit
#  boundaries are dead code, there is no seeding window in which OFF produces
#  durable rows: an OFF window would discard the entire seed. The requirement is
#  therefore satisfied in the only way the frozen source permits -- the loaders
#  run exactly as written, under the autocommit default the maintainer says they
#  normally get ("It is as default set ON"), which is also the mode his own
#  testing notes describe. The commit/rollback intention is preserved as a
#  reproduced legacy defect (R-4), not repaired: this image does not enable
#  transactional seeding that the frozen code cannot drive.
#
#  Set at SERVER level, not per session, so that every client inherits it
#  identically: the COBOL loaders, the COBOL bridges via `cobmysqlapi.o`, the
#  `mariadb` CLI in `seed.sh` / `reset_db.sh`, and the Python driver in
#  `dump_tables.py`. A per-session setting would let one side of the diff
#  differ from the other and turn this image into the comparison artefact that
#  R-6 forbids.
#
#  Declared EXPLICITLY rather than left to the upstream default. The base image
#  ships no autocommit setting anywhere under /etc/mysql, so the built-in
#  default of 1 would already apply -- but an explicit declaration makes the
#  setting assertable by `seed.sh`, `reset_db.sh` and `run_cobol_scenario.sh`,
#  and immune to a future base-image default change.
#
#  Written INTO the image rather than shipped as a separate `harness/my.cnf`:
#  the harness folder inventory is exactly eleven files plus `scenarios/`, and
#  a twelfth file is out of scope.
#
#  Placed in /etc/mysql/conf.d/ and named `99-` so it is read LAST and wins.
#  /etc/mysql/my.cnf includes `mariadb.conf.d/` BEFORE `conf.d/`, and its own
#  header states "If the same option is defined multiple times, the last one
#  will apply." Nothing else in the image sets autocommit, so this is the sole
#  definition; the `99-` prefix guarantees precedence even if that changes.
#
#  DELIBERATELY ABSENT from [mysqld], each for a specific reason:
#    * `sql_mode`                      -- the dump sets `NO_AUTO_VALUE_ON_ZERO`
#                                         itself, per session
#                                         [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L21]
#    * `character-set-server` /
#      `collation-server`              -- would override the schema's own
#                                         `utf8mb3` table defaults and thereby
#                                         "fix" the charset caveat at
#                                         [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L9-L11] (R-4)
#    * `PAD_CHAR_TO_FULL_LENGTH`       -- would change how the 238 fixed-width
#                                         `char` columns read back, and trailing
#                                         -space canonicalisation is
#                                         `normalize.py`'s job, not the server's
#    * `innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit`
#      and every other durability knob  -- changing durability changes commit
#                                         semantics, which is exactly what the
#                                         seeded and posted state depend on
#    * general / slow query log        -- writes timestamped output, i.e. a
#                                         non-deterministic artefact (R-6)
#    * `explicit_defaults_for_timestamp`
#                                      -- unnecessary: the 22 in-scope tables
#                                         contain zero TIMESTAMP columns
#    * any parallelism or pooling knob -- execution is strictly sequential and
#                                         there are no parallel scenario runs
#                                         against the shared database
#
#  NOTE for downstream scripts: with autocommit=1, every DML statement commits
#  as it completes, which is the only mode in which the frozen COBOL loaders and
#  bridges -- none of which reaches a COMMIT -- leave durable rows. `seed.sh`,
#  `reset_db.sh` and `run_cobol_scenario.sh` ASSERT this mode before doing work;
#  none of them ever SETS it, so this file remains the single authority.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    printf '%s\n' \
      '#' \
      '# ACAS compiled-COBOL oracle -- server configuration.' \
      '# Generated by harness/Dockerfile.mariadb. Do not edit in a running container.' \
      '#' \
      '# autocommit=1 is REQUIRED for the frozen COBOL to write durable rows.' \
      '#' \
      '# The loader banner at [common/glbatchLD.cbl:L9-L13] asks for OFF:' \
      '#   "This modules uses commit and rollback so you MUST ensure that autocommit' \
      '#    is OFF in the rdb settings. It is as default set ON."' \
      '# but the shipped code never carries that out. In all 28 common/*LD.cbl' \
      '# loaders every "perform aa020-Rollback" is commented out and aa030-Commit' \
      '# has zero perform sites, so both paragraphs are dead code:' \
      '#   grep -n "^ *perform.*\\(aa020\\|aa030\\|Commit\\|Rollback\\)" common/*LD.cbl' \
      '# returns no matches. The twenty in-scope bridges, the in-scope handlers' \
      '# (acas007, acas008, acasirsub4) and every bridge close path likewise' \
      '# contain zero COMMIT/ROLLBACK/START TRANSACTION, and cobmysqlapi38.c' \
      '# exposes MySQL_commit without ever calling it.' \
      '#' \
      '# Under autocommit=0 MariaDB would therefore discard every COBOL write --' \
      '# seeding and posting alike -- at session close, while the Python side' \
      '# commits, making the parity diff report a harness artefact on every row.' \
      '# The maintainer describes the same conclusion at [common/analLD.cbl:L442]:' \
      '#   "These do not work during testing with mariadb - Non transactional' \
      '#    model or autocommit set ON"' \
      '#' \
      '# Set server-wide so every client -- COBOL loaders, COBOL bridges via' \
      '# cobmysqlapi.o, the mariadb CLI, and the Python driver -- inherits it' \
      '# identically. Both sides of the parity diff must see the same server.' \
      '#' \
      '# Nothing else is set here by design: no sql_mode (the dump sets' \
      '# NO_AUTO_VALUE_ON_ZERO per session at [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L21]), no server' \
      '# charset/collation (that would "fix" the charset caveat at' \
      '# [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L9-L11], which must be preserved), no' \
      '# PAD_CHAR_TO_FULL_LENGTH, no durability change, no query logging.' \
      '#' \
      '[mysqld]' \
      'autocommit=1' \
      > /etc/mysql/conf.d/99-acas-oracle.cnf; \
    chmod 0644 /etc/mysql/conf.d/99-acas-oracle.cnf; \
    grep -qx 'autocommit=1' /etc/mysql/conf.d/99-acas-oracle.cnf


#  TARGET DATABASE
#  `ACASDB`, from [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L3] ("Host: localhost  Database: ACASDB").
#  This default is load-bearing: the frozen dump holds 33 `CREATE TABLE` and
#  zero `CREATE DATABASE`, zero `USE`, so the schema must already exist AND be
#  selected before the dump is replayed.
#  The vendor entrypoint does both when `MARIADB_DATABASE` is set -- it issues
#  `CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS` and `docker_exec_client` prepends
#  `--database="$MYSQL_DATABASE"` to every init-file run. That statement is the
#  vendor's and creates only the namespace the dump requires (no table, column
#  or index), so it is not schema evolution and is unavoidable.
#  Baked in as a default so a bare `docker run` is correct too; compose
#  supplies the same value and may override but must never diverge.
ENV MARIADB_DATABASE=ACASDB


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  DISPOSABILITY SENTINEL -- proof that this server is a harness throwaway
#
#  `harness/reset_db.sh` drops and re-applies all 33 tables. Before it does, it
#  demands positive proof that the server it is aimed at is one this harness
#  provisioned, and this schema is that proof: it exists ONLY in an image built
#  from this Dockerfile, so a shared or production server cannot carry it by
#  accident. Its ABSENCE makes the reset refuse.
#
#  Proof by PRESENCE of a marker, not by absence of production data, and the
#  distinction is the whole design. Any "does this look like production?" test is
#  a heuristic that fails open -- an empty staging database and an empty
#  production database are indistinguishable. A marker only this build writes
#  fails CLOSED: an unrecognised server is refused by default, and an operator
#  who genuinely means to reset one must say so explicitly and name it.
#
#  R-3 COMPLIANCE, stated plainly because this adds DDL and R-3 forbids schema
#  changes. What R-3 freezes is the ACAS schema: `mysql/ACASDB.sql` is copied
#  verbatim below and nothing here touches it, ACASDB still receives exactly the
#  33 frozen tables and no others, and no ACAS table, column, index or constraint
#  is added, altered or removed. This creates a SEPARATE schema that no migrated
#  code reads or writes, that no in-scope table lives in, and that never appears
#  in a table dump or a state diff -- it is oracle apparatus, in the same category
#  as the harness itself (AAP 0.7 C-1/C-2: harness apparatus is not the migrated
#  system). `reset_db.sh` also verifies that ACASDB holds no table outside the
#  frozen 33, and this sentinel is not in ACASDB, so it cannot perturb that check.
#
#  Named `05-` so it sorts BEFORE `10-ACASDB.sql`: the sentinel is established
#  before the frozen dump is replayed, so any later stage that reaches a
#  half-initialised server still finds the marker and behaves correctly.
#
#  Written from a heredoc-free printf, matching the configuration file above, so
#  the image is self-contained and this file is the only place the sentinel is
#  defined. No new repository file is created for it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    printf '%s\n' \
      '--' \
      '-- ACAS compiled-COBOL oracle -- harness disposability sentinel.' \
      '-- Generated by harness/Dockerfile.mariadb. NOT part of the ACAS schema.' \
      '--' \
      '-- harness/reset_db.sh requires this marker before it will drop and' \
      '-- re-apply the 33 frozen tables. It exists only in an image built from' \
      '-- this Dockerfile, so its presence proves the target is a harness-owned' \
      '-- throwaway rather than a shared or production server.' \
      '--' \
      '-- This is a SEPARATE schema. It contains no ACAS table, it is never' \
      '-- dumped, never normalized and never compared, and no migrated Python' \
      '-- module reads it. The frozen mysql/ACASDB.sql is untouched (R-3).' \
      '--' \
      'CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS `acas_harness_disposable`;' \
      'CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS `acas_harness_disposable`.`disposability_marker` (' \
      '  `marker` varchar(64) NOT NULL,' \
      '  `detail` varchar(255) NOT NULL,' \
      '  PRIMARY KEY (`marker`)' \
      ') ENGINE=InnoDB;' \
      "INSERT INTO \`acas_harness_disposable\`.\`disposability_marker\` (\`marker\`, \`detail\`)" \
      "  VALUES ('harness-owned-disposable', 'Built by harness/Dockerfile.mariadb. Every table in ACASDB may be dropped and re-applied from the frozen mysql/ACASDB.sql.')" \
      '  ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE `detail` = VALUES(`detail`);' \
      'COMMIT;' \
      > /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/05-acas-harness-disposable.sql; \
    chmod 0644 /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/05-acas-harness-disposable.sql; \
    grep -q 'acas_harness_disposable' /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/05-acas-harness-disposable.sql; \
    grep -q 'disposability_marker' /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/05-acas-harness-disposable.sql


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  THE FROZEN SCHEMA -- copied verbatim, never transformed (R-3)
#  Source path resolves against the REPOSITORY ROOT build context (see above).
#  Named `10-` so it sorts predictably within /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/,
#  which the vendor entrypoint replays in lexical order on first boot of an
#  empty data directory -- AFTER the `05-` sentinel above.
#
#  This is a plain COPY. There is no pipe, no filter, no rewrite and no
#  wrapper: the bytes in the image are the bytes in the repository.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
COPY mysql/ACASDB.sql /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/10-ACASDB.sql


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  VERBATIM GUARANTEE -- build-time tripwire for R-3 and R-4
#
#  R-3 forbids schema evolution of any kind and the AAP freezes
#  `mysql/ACASDB.sql` outright: "any diff touching ... mysql/ACASDB.sql is a
#  defect in the migration, regardless of how harmless it appears." Because the
#  file is frozen, pinning its digest cannot go stale -- the check fails
#  precisely, and only, when someone commits the violation the AAP forbids.
#  That makes the "applied byte-for-byte" claim MACHINE-CHECKED at build time
#  instead of merely asserted in a comment.
#
#  The digest below is of `mysql/ACASDB.sql` as committed: 1459 lines,
#  51008 bytes.
#
#  IT IS AN IMMUTABLE CONSTANT, NOT A BUILD ARGUMENT, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS
#  THE WHOLE POINT. An earlier revision of this file declared it as
#  `ARG ACASDB_SCHEMA_SHA256=...`, which made the tripwire self-defeating: a
#  build could modify the frozen schema and then pass
#  `--build-arg ACASDB_SCHEMA_SHA256=<digest of the modified file>` to make the
#  check pass, so the guarantee held only for builds that were not trying to
#  break it. A tripwire that the tripped party can re-arm is not a tripwire.
#  The expected digest is therefore a literal inside the RUN body, where no
#  build argument, environment variable or compose override can reach it, and
#  the step additionally REFUSES TO RUN if a variable of that name is visible at
#  all -- which catches both a re-introduced `ARG` above and an inherited `ENV`.
#  Re-pinning is still possible, but only the way the AAP intends: by editing
#  this file in a reviewable commit.
#
#  A note on why re-pinning is almost never the right response. The digest can
#  only mismatch if `mysql/ACASDB.sql` changed, and the AAP freezes that file
#  absolutely. The correct fix is always to revert the schema, never to update
#  the constant -- which is why the failure message says exactly that.
#
#  The five structural invariants that follow are READ-ONLY greps over the
#  COPYied file. They perform no transformation; they exist so that a failure
#  names the violated rule instead of only printing a digest mismatch. Each is
#  a fact independently verified against the committed schema.
#
#  Written deliberately WITHOUT any shell pipeline. /bin/sh here is dash, which
#  does not support `set -o pipefail`, so in a pipeline only the rightmost exit
#  status would be observed -- and a tripwire that can pass vacuously is worse
#  than no tripwire. The digest is therefore captured into a variable (so
#  `set -e` observes sha256sum's own failure) and trimmed with POSIX parameter
#  expansion, and the ALTER TABLE test uses a single anti-anchored pattern
#  instead of `grep | grep -v`.
#
#  On the ALTER TABLE pattern: a naive `grep -c 'ALTER TABLE'` reports 66 on
#  this dump, yet there is not one real ALTER TABLE in it -- every hit sits
#  inside a mysqldump version guard, `/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `X` DISABLE KEYS */`
#  [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L45]. `^[^/]*ALTER TABLE` matches only an occurrence with
#  no `/` before it on the line, so the 66 guarded hits are excluded while a
#  genuinely added statement -- at column 1 or indented -- is caught.
#
#  On the float test: it matches the bare TOKENS `float`, `double` and `real`
#  anywhere in the file rather than only in column position. That is the
#  stronger assertion -- the frozen schema contains zero occurrences of all
#  three words, in DDL and in comments alike -- and it cannot be evaded by an
#  unusual column declaration. `decimal` is unaffected, as it contains none of
#  the three as a whole word.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    schema=/docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/10-ACASDB.sql; \
    \
    if [ -n "${ACASDB_SCHEMA_SHA256:-}" ]; then \
        echo "FATAL: ACASDB_SCHEMA_SHA256 is visible to this step." >&2; \
        echo "       The frozen-schema digest is an IMMUTABLE CONSTANT in this Dockerfile," >&2; \
        echo "       never a build argument: an overrideable tripwire lets a modified" >&2; \
        echo "       mysql/ACASDB.sql be waved through with a matching --build-arg." >&2; \
        echo "       Remove the ARG/ENV of that name and re-pin by editing this file." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    \
    expected_sha=094e588226f650672c3781d1737d7bf8be30be048ce6a788df81800a19326ed0; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the pinned architecture actually took effect ---'; \
    arch="$(dpkg --print-architecture)"; \
    if [ "${arch}" != 'amd64' ]; then \
        echo "FATAL: this image is being built for ${arch}, not amd64." >&2; \
        echo "       The FROM line pins --platform=linux/amd64 so that both halves of" >&2; \
        echo "       the parity comparison run on the architecture the frozen dump's own" >&2; \
        echo "       header records -- [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L1] \"for Linux (x86_64)\"." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    echo "    dpkg architecture: ${arch}   uname -m: $(uname -m)"; \
    \
    echo '[R-3] verifying the frozen schema was copied byte-for-byte ...'; \
    actual_sha="$(sha256sum "${schema}")"; \
    actual_sha="${actual_sha%% *}"; \
    if [ "${actual_sha}" != "${expected_sha}" ]; then \
        echo "FATAL: mysql/ACASDB.sql is NOT byte-identical to the frozen baseline." >&2; \
        echo "       expected ${expected_sha}" >&2; \
        echo "       actual   ${actual_sha}" >&2; \
        echo "       The schema is frozen: any diff touching mysql/ACASDB.sql is a defect," >&2; \
        echo "       regardless of how harmless it appears. Revert it, do not re-pin." >&2; \
        exit 1; \
    fi; \
    \
    echo '[R-3] 33 CREATE TABLE statements, unchanged (22 in scope + 11 out of scope) ...'; \
    n_tables="$(grep -c '^CREATE TABLE ' "${schema}" || true)"; \
    [ "${n_tables}" = '33' ] || { echo "FATAL: expected 33 CREATE TABLE, found ${n_tables}." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '[R-3] zero real ALTER TABLE statements ...'; \
    n_alter="$(grep -cE '^[^/]*ALTER TABLE' "${schema}" || true)"; \
    [ "${n_alter}" = '0' ] || { echo "FATAL: ${n_alter} real ALTER TABLE statement(s) present." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '[R-3] zero CREATE INDEX / VIEW / TRIGGER / PROCEDURE statements ...'; \
    n_ddl="$(grep -ciE 'CREATE (INDEX|VIEW|TRIGGER|PROCEDURE|FUNCTION|DATABASE)' "${schema}" || true)"; \
    [ "${n_ddl}" = '0' ] || { echo "FATAL: ${n_ddl} added DDL statement(s) present." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '[R-4] charset caveat preserved: all 33 tables still utf8mb3_general_ci ...'; \
    n_cs="$(grep -c 'DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3 COLLATE=utf8mb3_general_ci' "${schema}" || true)"; \
    [ "${n_cs}" = '33' ] || { echo "FATAL: expected 33 utf8mb3_general_ci tables, found ${n_cs}; the charset caveat at [mysql/ACASDB.sql:L9-L11] must NOT be fixed." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '[R-2] zero FLOAT / DOUBLE / REAL tokens anywhere in the schema ...'; \
    n_float="$(grep -ciwE 'float|double|real' "${schema}" || true)"; \
    [ "${n_float}" = '0' ] || { echo "FATAL: ${n_float} line(s) carry a binary floating-point type; accounting values must never traverse a float." >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo 'OK: frozen schema verified verbatim -- 33 tables, 0 added DDL, utf8mb3 preserved, 0 float columns.'


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PRIVILEGE POSTURE -- LEAST PRIVILEGE, AND WHY THERE IS NO `USER` HERE
#
#  This image deliberately does NOT carry a `USER` instruction, and that is a
#  correctness requirement rather than an oversight. The vendor entrypoint
#  performs its OWN privilege drop: it starts as root, fixes the data directory
#  if required, and then re-executes itself under the unprivileged `mysql`
#  account (`exec gosu mysql "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}" "$@"`). Forcing `USER mysql`
#  here pre-empts that logic and breaks first boot on any data directory the
#  container does not already own -- notably a host bind mount, which Docker
#  does not re-own.
#
#  So the server ends up unprivileged either way; the question is only what the
#  BRIEFLY-root entrypoint is permitted to do. That is constrained from the
#  compose side, where it can be expressed without breaking the drop:
#
#      cap_drop:      [ALL]
#      cap_add:       [CHOWN, SETGID, SETUID, DAC_OVERRIDE, FOWNER]
#      security_opt:  [no-new-privileges:true]
#
#  Those values are MEASURED, not guessed. Probed on this stack against a fresh
#  named volume:
#
#      SETGID + SETUID only ................ starts and becomes healthy
#      + CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE, FOWNER ....... starts and becomes healthy
#      ALL dropped, nothing added .......... FAILS, and says exactly why:
#          "Switching to dedicated user 'mysql'"
#          "error: failed switching to \"mysql\": operation not permitted"
#
#  SETGID and SETUID are therefore the irreducible pair -- they are what the
#  privilege drop itself consumes. CHOWN, DAC_OVERRIDE and FOWNER are retained
#  on top because the entrypoint chowns the data directory when it does not
#  already own it; a fresh NAMED volume inherits ownership from this image and
#  so does not need them, but a bind-mounted directory does, and failing on a
#  bind mount would be a fixture trap rather than a security gain. Every
#  NETWORK and KERNEL capability the default set would grant -- NET_RAW,
#  NET_BIND_SERVICE, NET_ADMIN, MKNOD, SYS_CHROOT, KILL, AUDIT_WRITE -- is
#  dropped and none is added back. After the drop the server process holds
#  `CapEff: 0000000000000000`, verified in the same probe.
#
#  SETUID/SETGID BINARIES ARE STRIPPED
#  -----------------------------------
#  The base image inherits the distribution's usual set-user-ID helpers, none of
#  which this container's function requires: it serves one database and never
#  changes user, mounts a filesystem or edits an account. They are cleared so
#  that a process which somehow gains file-system access cannot use one of them
#  to regain privilege. This is defence in depth alongside
#  `no-new-privileges:true`, which already neuters the set-user-ID bit at the
#  kernel level -- both are kept because they fail independently.
#
#  Two things are deliberately NOT stripped:
#
#    * `gosu` -- verified NOT to be set-user-ID. It works because the
#      entrypoint is already root, so clearing bits elsewhere cannot break the
#      privilege drop. This was checked before stripping, not assumed.
#    * the vendor's own `auth_pam_tool` under /usr/lib/mysql/plugin/ -- a
#      MariaDB component. ACAS authenticates with native MariaDB accounts and
#      never PAM, but reaching into a vendor plugin directory is over-reach for
#      a fixture image and risks a subtle, hard-to-diagnose failure. It is left
#      exactly as shipped and recorded here so the decision is visible.
#
#  The assertion below fails the build if the privilege-drop machinery is not
#  intact, so a future base-image change cannot quietly remove it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
RUN set -eu; \
    echo '--- asserting the unprivileged service account exists ---'; \
    id mysql; \
    mysql_uid="$(id -u mysql)"; \
    [ "${mysql_uid}" != '0' ] \
      || { echo 'FATAL: the mysql account resolves to uid 0; the entrypoint privilege drop would be a no-op.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- asserting the privilege-drop helper is present ---'; \
    command -v gosu > /dev/null \
      || { echo 'FATAL: gosu is absent; the vendor entrypoint cannot drop privileges and the server would run as root.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- clearing set-user-ID / set-group-ID bits on distribution helpers ---'; \
    find / -xdev -perm /6000 -type f \
         -not -path '/usr/lib/mysql/plugin/*' \
         -print -exec chmod -s {} + ; \
    \
    echo '--- verifying only the vendor plugin retains a set-*-ID bit ---'; \
    remaining="$(find / -xdev -perm /6000 -type f -not -path '/usr/lib/mysql/plugin/*' | wc -l)"; \
    [ "${remaining}" = '0' ] \
      || { echo "FATAL: ${remaining} set-user-ID/set-group-ID binaries remain outside the vendor plugin directory." >&2; \
           find / -xdev -perm /6000 -type f -not -path '/usr/lib/mysql/plugin/*' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo '--- gosu must still be usable after the strip ---'; \
    gosu mysql id \
      || { echo 'FATAL: gosu can no longer switch to the mysql account; the privilege drop is broken.' >&2; exit 1; }; \
    \
    echo "OK: unprivileged account mysql (uid ${mysql_uid}) present, gosu intact, set-*-ID helpers cleared."


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  NETWORK
#
#  Compose service name `mariadb`; the other harness members reach this server
#  at hostname `mariadb` on port 3306 over TCP.
#
#  The client-side contract they use is:
#      ACAS_DB_HOST=mariadb      ACAS_DB_PORT=3306      ACAS_DB_NAME=ACASDB
#      ACAS_DB_USER / ACAS_DB_PASSWORD   (from compose; never defaulted here)
#      ACAS_DB_SOCKET=""         (empty => TCP)
#  Those `ACAS_DB_*` variables are consumed by the harness shell and Python
#  tools, NOT by this server image, so they are documented here for agreement
#  and deliberately not set: this image is configured through the `MARIADB_*`
#  set alone, and defaulting `ACAS_DB_PASSWORD` to anything would put a
#  credential in an image layer.
#
#  The data directory is the vendor default, /var/lib/mysql. `reset_db.sh`
#  drops and re-applies the frozen schema rather than deleting the volume, so
#  no special data-directory handling is required here.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
EXPOSE 3306


#  READINESS -- "healthy" MEANS "the frozen schema is fully applied"
#  `seed.sh`, `reset_db.sh` and both scenario runners must wait
#  DETERMINISTICALLY; a race here would corrupt the parity evidence.
#  The vendor `healthcheck.sh` is strictly READ-ONLY: `--connect` runs
#  `select 1`, `--innodb_initialized` reads engine status, and `--su-mysql`
#  runs both as the `mysql` unix user -- so no credential is needed or
#  embedded, and nothing is written, created or touched.
#  `--connect` probes over TCP while the entrypoint's temporary init server
#  runs `--skip-networking`, so the container cannot report healthy until
#  every /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/ script has finished.
#  `start-period` absorbs first-boot init without counting failures; every
#  value is a fixed constant, identical for the COBOL and the Python run.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=5s --timeout=10s --start-period=30s --retries=24 \
    CMD healthcheck.sh --su-mysql --connect --innodb_initialized


#  ENTRYPOINT / CMD are INHERITED, never overridden.
#  The vendor entrypoint is what initialises the data directory, creates and
#  selects `ACASDB`, replays /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d/ on first boot, drops
#  privileges to the `mysql` user and then execs the server. Overriding it
#  would bypass the schema application this image exists to perform.
#  On a restart with an existing data directory the entrypoint skips the init
#  scripts, so the frozen schema is applied exactly once and restarts are
#  idempotent.
